WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.980
A growing axon has to find one partner among many cells.

2
00:00:07.860 --> 00:00:11.100
The work is done at its tip, a moving fan called the growth cone.

3
00:00:11.980 --> 00:00:13.480
The growth cone feels its way.

4
00:00:14.500 --> 00:00:18.160
Molecules around it, called guidance cues, attract it or repel it.

5
00:00:18.920 --> 00:00:22.320
When a cue reaches one side more strongly, the growth cone turns.

6
00:00:23.320 --> 00:00:25.920
Turn by turn, it steers the axon to its target.

7
00:00:26.780 --> 00:00:28.920
So how does a tip move, and how does it turn?

8
00:00:29.900 --> 00:00:31.240
First, the evidence.

9
00:00:32.240 --> 00:00:37.140
Gundersen and Barrett placed a pipette releasing nerve growth factor near the axons

10
00:00:37.140 --> 00:00:38.520
of chick sensory neurons.

11
00:00:39.360 --> 00:00:43.560
The side of each growth cone nearer the pipette met more of it, and the growth cones

12
00:00:43.560 --> 00:00:49.040
turned toward it. Campbell and Holt then cut growth cones from frog retinal neurons

13
00:00:49.040 --> 00:00:50.520
away from their cell bodies.

14
00:00:51.440 --> 00:00:54.060
The isolated tips still turned to guidance cues.

15
00:00:55.000 --> 00:00:56.540
The decision is made at the tip.

16
00:00:57.533 --> 00:01:01.073
Now open the fan. Microtubules fill its centre.

17
00:01:02.153 --> 00:01:04.713
Actin fills the edge, and the actin is never still.

18
00:01:05.593 --> 00:01:07.773
New actin joins the filaments at the front.

19
00:01:08.853 --> 00:01:12.873
Motor proteins called myosin II pull the whole network back toward the centre.

20
00:01:13.733 --> 00:01:16.053
That backward movement is retrograde flow.

21
00:01:16.853 --> 00:01:20.953
If actin is added at the front and carried back at the same rate, the edge goes

22
00:01:20.953 --> 00:01:24.373
nowhere. The engine runs, but the car stays put.

23
00:01:25.417 --> 00:01:30.397
Now add a clutch. When adhesion molecules link the actin network to the surface

24
00:01:30.397 --> 00:01:35.457
under it, retrograde flow slows, and the same actin addition pushes the edge

25
00:01:35.457 --> 00:01:40.217
forward. Tim Mitchison and Marc Kirschner proposed this in 1988.

26
00:01:41.417 --> 00:01:46.837
Put it as a rule. The rate of advance equals the rate of actin addition, minus the

27
00:01:46.837 --> 00:01:48.177
rate of retrograde flow.

28
00:01:49.200 --> 00:01:51.820
Turning is that rule, applied unequally.

29
00:01:52.580 --> 00:01:57.780
A cue on one side shifts actin assembly, flow or adhesion there, so one side

30
00:01:57.780 --> 00:01:59.080
advances more than the other.

31
00:02:00.300 --> 00:02:03.000
Drugs released onto one side give simple results.

32
00:02:03.860 --> 00:02:07.760
Remove actin on one side, and the growth cone turns away from that side.

33
00:02:09.253 --> 00:02:10.353
Microtubules steer too.

34
00:02:11.213 --> 00:02:14.873
Stabilize them on one side, and the growth cone turns toward that side.

35
00:02:15.753 --> 00:02:18.093
Break them down on one side, and it turns away.

36
00:02:19.033 --> 00:02:24.073
As the edge moves, microtubules extend into the new territory, the fan behind them

37
00:02:24.073 --> 00:02:26.773
narrows, and that stretch becomes axon shaft.

38
00:02:27.793 --> 00:02:32.953
Keep the rule. The tip senses a cue, and turning is the difference between its two

39
00:02:32.953 --> 00:02:33.293
sides.
